The patch code for CVE-2019-15924 involves adding error handling to the `fm10k_init_module` function to check the return value of `alloc_workqueue`. If `alloc_workqueue` fails, the code should handle the error by cleaning up resources and returning an appropriate error code. Here is a general outline of what the patch might look like:

```c
static int __init fm10k_init_module(void)
{
    pr_info("%s - version %s\n", fm10k_driver_string, fm10k_driver_version);
    pr_info("%s\n", fm10k_copyright);

    /* create driver workqueue */
    fm10k_workqueue = alloc_workqueue("%s", WQ_MEM_RECLAIM, 0, fm10k_driver_name);
    if (!fm10k_workqueue) {
        pr_err("Failed to allocate workqueue\n");
        return -ENOMEM;
    }

    fm10k_dbg_init();

    int ret = fm10k_register_pci_driver();
    if (ret) {
        fm10k_dbg_exit();
        destroy_workqueue(fm10k_workqueue);
    }

    return ret;
}
```

This patch ensures that if `alloc_workqueue` fails, the function logs an error and returns `-ENOMEM`. Additionally, if `fm10k_register_pci_driver` fails, it cleans up by calling `fm10k_dbg_exit` and `destroy_workqueue`.

